Some new requirements have come up to install and configure some packages on the Nautilus infrastructure under Stratos Datacenter. The Nautilus DevOps team installed and configured a new Jenkins server so they wanted to create a Jenkins job to automate this task. Find below more details and complete the task accordingly:
1. Access the Jenkins UI by clicking on the Jenkins button in the top bar. Log in using the credentials: username admin and password Adm!n321.
2. Create a new Jenkins job named install-packages and configure it with the following specifications:
Add a string parameter named PACKAGE.
Configure the job to install a package specified in the $PACKAGE parameter on the storage server (Stratos Datacenter).
Build the job at least once (e.g. with parameter PACKAGE=vim-enhanced) so the package is installed on the Storage server and can be verified.

Note:

1. Ensure to install any required plugins and restart the Jenkins service if necessary. Opt for Restart Jenkins when installation is complete and no jobs are running on the plugin installation/update page. Refresh the UI page if needed after restarting the service.
2. Verify that the Jenkins job runs successfully on repeated executions to ensure reliability.
3. Capture screenshots of your configuration for documentation and review purposes. Alternatively, use screen recording software like loom.com for comprehensive documentation and sharing.

SOLUTION
This task can be completed entirely from the Jenkins UI. Follow these steps:

1. Open Jenkins
Click Jenkins in the top bar.
Log in:
Username: admin
Password: Adm!n321
2. Create the install-packages job
From the Jenkins dashboard, click New Item.
Enter:
install-packages
Select Freestyle project.
Click OK.
3. Add the PACKAGE parameter

In the job configuration:

Under General, tick This project is parameterized.
Click Add Parameter → String Parameter.
Set:
Name: PACKAGE
Description: Package to install on the storage server
Default value can be left blank.
Save the parameter configuration.
4. Configure the build command

Scroll to Build Steps → Add build step → Execute shell.

Use SSH to connect to the storage server and install the requested package:

sshpass -p '<storage-password>' ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no <storage-user>@ststor01 "sudo yum install -y $PACKAGE"

However, don't guess the storage-server username/password. In these Nautilus labs, the credentials can vary.

If Jenkins already has an SSH key configured for the storage server, use:

ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no <storage-user>@ststor01 "sudo yum install -y $PACKAGE"

The important part is that $PACKAGE comes from the Jenkins string parameter.

5. If SSH/credentials are not available

If the build fails with something such as:

Permission denied

or

ssh: command not found

check the Jenkins server first:

which ssh
which sshpass

If sshpass is required and permitted:

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y sshpass

Then use the appropriate storage-server credentials supplied by the lab.

6. Build the job

Go back to the install-packages job:

Click Build with Parameters.
Enter:
PACKAGE=vim-enhanced
Click Build.
Open the build number → Console Output.
Confirm that the installation completes successfully.

You should see something along the lines of:

Complete!
Finished: SUCCESS
7. Verify on the storage server

SSH to the storage server and run:

rpm -q vim-enhanced

or:

yum list installed vim-enhanced

It should show the installed package.

8. Test repeated executions

Run Build with Parameters again with:

PACKAGE=vim-enhanced

The second execution should also finish successfully. yum install -y should recognise that the package is already installed rather than failing.

Important: Don't hard-code vim-enhanced in the build command. The command must use $PACKAGE, because the lab is specifically testing the Jenkins parameterisation.

